  "before_interfaces": {
    "def-potential-outcomes-and-consistency": [
      "## Definition\n\nFix a target population with probability law $P$, a real outcome, and a nonempty countable discrete set $\\mathcal A$ of treatment versions. A treatment version $a\\in\\mathcal A$ is a specified intervention protocol, not just a treatment label. The **potential outcome** $Y^a$ is the real random variable giving the outcome for a unit in the target population under version $a$. Write $A$ for the observed treatment assignment or receipt, with values in $\\mathcal A$, and $Y$ for the observed outcome. The **observed potential outcome** is the measurable random variable\n\n$$Y^A=\\sum_{a\\in\\mathcal A}\\mathbf 1_{\\{A=a\\}}Y^a,$$\n\nwhere the countable sum has at most one nonzero summand at each sample point. **Consistency** is the assumption $Y=Y^A$ $P$-almost surely. It links the observed outcome to the potential outcome for the received version; it does not assert that treatment assignment is unconfounded.\n\nThe **full-data law** is the joint law of the potential-outcome family, $A$, any baseline covariate random element $L$, and $Y$. The **observed-data law** is its marginal law of $(A,L,Y)$. A target population, outcome, intervention protocol, and contrast must be fixed before the associated causal target is specified. If interference between units matters, the version or treatment regime must include the relevant assignment or exposure of other units; the notation $Y^a$ by itself does not assert no interference."
    ],
    "def-positivity-for-discrete-treatment": [
      "## Definition\n\nLet $A$ take values in a countable discrete set on a target probability space, and let $L$ be the baseline covariate random element. For a treatment value $a$ of interest, the conditional treatment probability is the almost-sure class\n\n$$e_a=E[\\mathbf 1_{\\{A=a\\}}\\mid\\sigma(L)],$$\n\nusing the conditional-expectation convention of [[def-conditional-expectation-as-an-ae-class]] under the [[def-axiom-of-choice]]. **Positivity for $a$ on the target population** means both $P(A=a)>0$ and $e_a>0$ almost surely under the target law of $L$. Positivity for a contrast is required at every treatment value in that contrast.\n\nThe almost-sure condition is relative to the target population: values of $L$ with zero target probability impose no condition. Strict positivity does not mean that $e_a$ is bounded away from zero. The marginal condition makes the observed treatment-stratum law $P(\\,\\cdot\\mid A=a)$ available."
    ],
    "lem-conditional-independence-integrable-factorization": [
      "## Statement\n\nAssume the [[def-axiom-of-choice]] for the conditional-expectation classes. Let $U$ be a real integrable random variable, let $Z$ be a random element, and let $\\mathcal G$ be a sub-sigma-algebra. If $U$ and $Z$ are conditionally independent given $\\mathcal G$ in the bounded-function sense of [[def-conditional-independence-given-a-sigma-algebra]], then for every bounded measurable real function $h$ on the state space of $Z$,\n\n$$E[U h(Z)\\mid\\mathcal G]=E[U\\mid\\mathcal G]E[h(Z)\\mid\\mathcal G]\\quad\\text{almost surely}.$$"
    ],
    "def-conditional-exchangeability-of-potential-outcomes": [
      "## Definition\n\nLet $A$ be the observed treatment assignment, $L$ a baseline covariate random element, and $(Y^a)_{a\\in\\mathcal A}$ the potential-outcome family of [[def-potential-outcomes-and-consistency]]. **Conditional exchangeability given $L$** means\n\n$$\\sigma(Y^a:a\\in\\mathcal A)\\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp\\sigma(A)\\mid\\sigma(L),$$\n\nwhere conditional independence is the bounded-measurable-function factorization of [[def-conditional-independence-given-a-sigma-algebra]] under the [[def-axiom-of-choice]]. Each fixed $Y^a$ is then conditionally independent of $A$ given $L$ by measurable-map preservation in [[lem-conditional-independence-equivalences-and-preservation]]. This is a full-data assumption; the observed distribution alone does not establish it."
    ],
    "def-causal-average-treatment-effects": [
      "## Definition\n\nIn a fixed target population with two specified treatment versions $0$ and $1$ and integrable potential outcomes $Y^0,Y^1$ from [[def-potential-outcomes-and-consistency]], the **average treatment effect (ATE)** is the target-population mean contrast $E[Y^1-Y^0]$. For a baseline covariate random element $L$, the **conditional average treatment effect (CATE) given $L$** is the conditional-expectation almost-sure class\n\n$$E[Y^1-Y^0\\mid\\sigma(L)],$$\n\nusing the conditional-expectation convention of [[def-conditional-expectation-as-an-ae-class]] under the [[def-axiom-of-choice]]. Thus a CATE is determined only up to equality almost surely under the target law of $L$; a pointwise value at a null covariate value is not specified by this definition. The ATE is the mean of the individual contrast, not an assertion that both potential outcomes are observed for any unit."
    ],
    "thm-discrete-treatment-potential-outcome-mean-identification": [
      "## Statement\n\nAssume the Axiom of Choice. Let $\\mathcal A$ be a countable discrete set containing treatment versions $0$ and $1$. On a full-data probability space $(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,P)$, let $A$ take values in $\\mathcal A$, let $L$ take values in a standard-Borel space $\\mathcal L$ ([[def-standard-borel-space]]), let $Y$ be the observed real outcome, and let $(Y^b)_{b\\in\\mathcal A}$ be the potential outcomes of [[def-potential-outcomes-and-consistency]]. For each treatment value $a$ under consideration, assume $Y^a\\in L^1(P)$, consistency $Y=Y^A$ almost surely, conditional exchangeability $\\sigma(Y^b:b\\in\\mathcal A)\\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp\\sigma(A)\\mid\\sigma(L)$, and positivity $P(A=a)>0$ and $E[\\mathbf1_{\\{A=a\\}}\\mid\\sigma(L)]>0$ almost surely. These are assumptions on the full-data law; Choice is required for the stated conditional-expectation classes and regular conditional distributions.\n\nWrite $P_L(B)=P(L\\in B)$ for the target-population law of $L$. For such an $a$, define the observed treatment-stratum law $P_a(C)=P(C\\cap\\{A=a\\})/P(A=a)$. Under $P_a$, let $K_a(l,dy)$ be a regular conditional law of $Y$ given the standard-Borel random element $L$, and put $m_a(l)=\\int y\\,K_a(l,dy)$ wherever the absolute integral is finite, with value $0$ on the exceptional set. The suppliers [[def-conditional-law-given-a-random-element]], [[thm-existence-of-regular-conditional-distributions-for-standard-borel-targets]], [[lem-regular-conditional-kernels-factor-through-a-standard-borel-conditioning-variable]], and [[thm-conditional-integration-through-a-regular-conditional-law]] give this construction. Then $m_a$ is $P_L$-integrable and is uniquely determined $P_L$-almost everywhere by the observed-data law, and\n\n$$E_P[Y^a]=\\int_{\\mathcal L}m_a(l)\\,P_L(dl).$$\n\nIf $L$ is countably discrete, this is\n\n$$E_P[Y^a]=\\sum_{l:P(L=l)>0}E_P[Y\\mid A=a,L=l]P(L=l).$$\n\nIn this sum, $E[Y\\mid A=a,L=l]$ denotes $E[Y\\mathbf1_{\\{A=a,L=l\\}}]/P(A=a,L=l)$, whose denominator is positive for each displayed atom.\n\nFor the binary contrast, both treatment levels must satisfy positivity. The CATE is then represented $P_L$-almost everywhere by $m_1-m_0$, and the ATE is $\\int(m_1-m_0)\\,dP_L$. In the model whose parameters are all full-data laws satisfying these assumptions and whose observation map sends a full-data law to its $(A,L,Y)$ marginal, each $E[Y^a]$ is identifiable in the sense of [[def-identifiability-of-an-estimand]]: it is constant over full-data laws with the same observed-data law."
    ],
